{
  "id": "Compound::DB00881 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Quinapril may be associated with Hypertension (disease) through a chain of causal relationships involving Benazepril and Captopril, both of which are classified as Ace Inhibitors and Ammonium Ion Derivatives. These medications are known to treat hypertension by inhibiting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, thereby reducing blood pressure. The evidence from multiple paths indicates that Quinapril, through its interaction with Benazepril or Captopril, may contribute to the pathophysiology of hypertension, although the exact mechanism remains unclear. The low scores in Path 3 suggest a weaker relationship compared to the others, possibly due to differences in the causal pathways or the strength of the evidence. The integration of these findings implies that Quinapril could potentially be repurposed for hypertension treatment, though further research is needed to confirm this relationship and establish its clinical relevance.",
